column2:
Touch the man; let him wake;
217	let him go back in peace the road he came;
218	let him return to his land through the gate he left.

C[ome, Gilgam]eš — count your loaves,
236	[and the days you sle]pt shall be made known to you:
237	your [first] loaf [is dried out;]
238	the second is [leath]ery; the third is soggy;
239	your fourth crust has gone white;
240	the fifth has thrown its bloom of mould; the sixth is fresh-baked;
241	[the seventh — still o]n the coals — and at that you awoke.

Ur-šanabi — may the qu[ay c]ast you off; may the ferry-crossing detest you;
249	you who walked its shore, be shut from its shore.
250	The man you led here —
251	matted filth has fastened on his body;
252	pelts have ruined the beauty of his flesh.
253	Take him, Ur-šanabi; bring him to the washtub;
254	let him wash his matted hair clean as snow(?) in water;
255	let him throw off his pelts, and the sea carry them away;
256	let his body be rinsed to fairness;
257	let the kerchief of his head be made new;
258	let him be clothed in a robe, the garment of his dignity.
259	Until he goes home to his city,
260	until he arrives at the end of his road,
261	let the robe show no stain, but stay new and yet new.
